## 2. SDN overlay, underlay and fabric

| Layer | What it is | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| Underlay | The physical network and its IP routing; provides reachability between fabric nodes | Roads and railway tracks |
| Overlay | A virtual network built on top using tunnels; carries user traffic with segmentation | Bus routes drawn on top of the roads; the sealed parcel carried inside |
| Fabric | Underlay + overlay together, managed as one logical network | The whole transport system run as one service |

Underlay facts:
- Simple, stable and routed. It runs a routing protocol (IS-IS, OSPF or BGP).
- It knows nothing about users or tenants. It only moves tunnel packets between endpoints.

Overlay facts:
- Tunnel types: VXLAN, LISP, GRE, IPsec.
- Edge devices encapsulate on entry and decapsulate on exit.
- The underlay can change without disturbing the overlay.

VXLAN:
- Wraps a Layer 2 frame in UDP, port 4789.
- 24-bit VNI gives about 16 million segments, compared with 4094 VLANs.

Cisco SD-Access (campus fabric):

| Plane | Technology |
|---|---|
| Control plane | LISP |
| Data plane | VXLAN |
| Policy plane | Cisco TrustSec (security group tags, SGT) |

| Fabric role | Function |
|---|---|
| Edge node | Connects endpoints, like an access switch |
| Border node | Connects the fabric to outside networks |
| Control plane node | Tracks where each endpoint is, via the LISP map-server |
| Intermediate node | Underlay-only transit |
| Fabric WLC | Wireless integration |

Catalyst Center is the controller, and ISE provides policy and identity.

Data center version: Cisco ACI uses the APIC controller, a spine-leaf underlay and a VXLAN overlay.

