## 3. AI and machine learning in network operations

Hierarchy: AI ⊃ machine learning ⊃ deep learning.

| | Predictive AI | Generative AI |
|---|---|---|
| Does | Forecasts or classifies from data | Creates new content (text, code, config) |
| Network uses | Failure prediction, capacity planning, anomaly detection, threat detection | Natural-language queries, config and script drafts, log and incident summaries, documentation |
| Analogy | Weather forecast from past patterns | A junior assistant who drafts; you must review |
| Main risk | Wrong predictions from poor data | Hallucination (confident but wrong output) |

ML types:

| Type | Learns from | Network example |
|---|---|---|
| Supervised | Labeled data | Classify traffic as normal or attack |
| Unsupervised | Unlabeled data | Find unusual behavior without prior examples |
| Reinforcement | Rewards and penalties | Tune wireless channel and power settings |

Typical uses:
- Anomaly detection: the system learns what normal looks like, then flags deviations. Analogy: a bank fraud alert that learns your usual spending.
- Root-cause analysis: correlating thousands of events into one cause.
- Closed-loop automation: detect, decide, fix, verify.
- Wireless optimization: automatic channel and power adjustment.

Data sources: streaming telemetry, syslog, SNMP, NetFlow. Good models need good data.

Cautions:
- Always have a human validate AI-generated configs before deployment.
- Never paste confidential configs, credentials or customer data into public AI tools.
- Watch for bias, drift and limited explainability.

Chapters 14.1-14.3 of the Bangladesh Bank guideline cover AI/ML security risks and management.

