## 4. REST APIs

What: REST is an architectural style for APIs over HTTP.

Analogy: a restaurant. The menu is the API documentation, the waiter is the API, and the kitchen is the server. The waiter doesn't remember your last visit, so every order includes your table number (stateless).

REST characteristics: client-server, stateless, cacheable, uniform interface, layered system, resources identified by URIs.

### CRUD to HTTP verbs

| CRUD | HTTP verb | Use |
|---|---|---|
| Create | POST | Add a new resource |
| Read | GET | Retrieve (safe, no changes) |
| Update | PUT / PATCH | PUT replaces the whole resource; PATCH changes part of it |
| Delete | DELETE | Remove |

GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent (repeating them gives the same result). POST is not.

### URI structure
```
https://api.example.com:443/v1/devices/sw1?status=up
 scheme   host           port path          query
```

### Request and response
- Request: method, URI, headers (`Authorization`, `Content-Type`, `Accept`), optional body.
- Response: status code, headers, body (usually JSON).

### Status codes

| Class | Meaning | Common codes |
|---|---|---|
| 1xx | Informational | |
| 2xx | Success | 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content |
| 3xx | Redirect | 301, 302 |
| 4xx | Client error | 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (not authenticated), 403 Forbidden (authenticated but not permitted), 404 Not Found, 405 Method Not Allowed, 429 Too Many Requests |
| 5xx | Server error | 500 Internal Server Error, 503 Service Unavailable |

### Authentication

| Method | Note |
|---|---|
| Basic | Base64 `user:password`. Encoded, not encrypted, so use HTTPS |
| Token (Bearer) | Obtained once, sent with each request |
| API key | Static key in a header or query |
| OAuth | Delegated, scoped access |

### Example
```
curl -X GET "https://controller.example.com/api/v1/devices?status=up" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer <TOKEN>" \
  -H "Accept: application/json"
```

Data encodings: JSON (most common), XML, YAML.

Traps:
- 401 means *who are you*, and 403 means *you may not*.
- POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH changes part.
- Basic auth over plain HTTP exposes the password.

